WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.110
A problem statement is a canvas, not a slogan.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.530
Fill it before you decorate an offer.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:07.949
This lesson is about problem statement worksheet.

00:00:07.949 --> 00:00:10.368
Who hurts, how often, what they tried.

00:00:10.368 --> 00:00:12.787
Do not treat this as theory only.

00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:16.243
Treat it as a move you can run this week.

00:00:16.243 --> 00:00:21.427
By the end, you should be able to act without waiting for a perfect plan.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:25.229
Keep the DigProg habit: watch, practice, then take one real action.

00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:29.376
Stay practical: fill the problem canvas before you optimize branding or tools.

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:35.169
Who hurts, how often, what they tried, and what it costs them if nothing changes.

00:00:35.169 --> 00:00:37.723
Remember the objective: fill the problem canvas.

00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:41.736
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:46.115
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:00:46.115 --> 00:00:50.128
Avoid vanity work that feels busy but does not create proof.

00:00:50.128 --> 00:00:54.507
If something is unclear, simplify the promise and try a smaller version.

00:00:54.507 --> 00:00:57.061
Talk to a real person when possible.

00:00:57.061 --> 00:00:58.520
Feedback beats guessing alone.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.358
